IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `cacl-passenger`

-- Civil Aviation (Carriers' Liability) Regulations 2019 -- the figures the Regulations prescribe for Part IV of the Act, read
-- from the deposited instrument instead of assumed. A module beside cacl-passenger.l4; it calls that module's `Limit` and
-- `the higher of`, and restates no rule of the Act.
--
-- SEAT: the air passenger, as in cacl-passenger.l4. "Have the Regulations raised the limit on what the airline can be made to
-- pay me, for my injury or my bags, above the Act's own figures?"
--
-- Edition: the Regulations as made (register id F2019L01172, made 5 September 2019, in force 1 October 2019; unamended, no
-- compilation registered), deposited at
-- ../../../civil-aviation-carriers-liability-regulations-2019/registers/source-bundle/F2019L01172.txt (retrieved 2026-10-23).
-- They repealed the Civil Aviation (Carriers' Liability) Regulations 1991 (Schedule 1), the instrument REQ-0089 named.
--
-- WHAT THE REGULATIONS PRESCRIBE (Part 2): s 6(1) $925,000 "for the purposes of subsection 31(1)"; s 6(2) 480,000 SDRs for
-- s 31(1A); s 7(1) $3,000 for s 31(2); s 7(2) $300 for s 31(3). Each is equal to the Act's own figure in s 31(1AA), (1B),
-- (2A), (4), so none is "higher than" it and paragraph (b) of each subsection never takes over (NOTES.md finding 11).
-- WHAT THEY DO NOT: no number of SDRs for s 9C(1), s 11A(1)(b) or s 21A(1)(b) (an Australian international carrier's limit);
-- no indexation for s 31(1AA)(b), (1B)(b), (2A)(b), (4)(b); no ticket or baggage check provisions under s 40, so nothing
-- displaces a s 31 limit; no family member classes under s 5(2)(f). Parts 3 and 4 (insurance, CASA audits) are addressed to
-- carriers, insurers and CASA and are out of this seat's scope.
--
-- NOT IN THE REGULATIONS: the Article 24 revisions of the 1999 Montreal Convention limits (s 5(1), para (a) of the
-- definition). They are notified by ICAO, whose website terms do not allow redistribution, so they are not deposited and the
-- Montreal answers in cacl-passenger.l4 stay on the Convention text's figures (NOTES.md finding 12; the new ledger entry).

§ `Civil Aviation (Carriers' Liability) Regulations 2019 -- the passenger's limits`

§§ `ss 6, 7 -- the figures prescribed for s 31`

-- reg 6(1): "For the purposes of subsection 31(1) of the Act, the amount prescribed is $925,000."
`the amount prescribed for s 31(1)` MEANS 925000
-- reg 6(2): "For the purposes of subsection 31(1A) of the Act, the number of SDRs prescribed is 480,000."
`the SDRs prescribed for s 31(1A)` MEANS 480000
-- reg 7(1): "For the purposes of subsection 31(2) of the Act, the amount prescribed is $3,000."
`the amount prescribed for s 31(2)` MEANS 3000
-- reg 7(2): "For the purposes of subsection 31(3) of the Act, the amount prescribed is $300."
`the amount prescribed for s 31(3)` MEANS 300

-- s 31(1AA)(a), (1B)(a), (2A)(a), (4)(a): the Act's own figures, which apply "unless paragraph (b) applies" -- that is, unless
-- the regulations provide for indexation. The 2019 Regulations provide for none.
`the s 31(1AA) amount` MEANS 925000
`the s 31(1B) number` MEANS 480000
`the s 31(2A) amount` MEANS 3000
`the s 31(4) amount` MEANS 300

-- s 31(1): the limit is (a) the (1AA) amount; (b) the prescribed amount, where it "is higher than the amount applicable under
-- subsection (1AA)" and (c) does not apply; (c) with no (b) regulation, a higher amount the contract of carriage specifies;
-- (d) with a (b) regulation, a contract amount that exceeds the prescribed amount. In each case the larger figure wins, so the
-- limit is the highest of the Act's figure, a prescribed figure that exceeds it, and a contract figure that exceeds both.
GIVEN `the Act's figure` IS A NUMBER
      `the prescribed figure` IS A NUMBER
      `the contract's figure` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the s 31 limit from` MEANS
    `the higher of` (`the higher of` `the Act's figure` `the prescribed figure`) `the contract's figure`

GIVEN `the Act's figure` IS A NUMBER
      `the prescribed figure` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the regulation raises the figure` IF `the prescribed figure` GREATER THAN `the Act's figure`

DECLARE `Part IV carrier` IS ONE OF
    -- s 26: a domestic carrier is one that operates the carriage as domestic carriage under s 27(1)(a)-(c)
    `a domestic carrier, for carriage between States or to, from or within a Territory`
    `another Part IV carrier, for carriage between Australia and another country that no Convention covers`

@export Under the Regulations in force, what is the most the airline can be made to pay for my death or injury on a Part IV flight (s 31(1), (1A); regs 6(1), (2))?
GIVEN `which carriage` IS A `Part IV carrier` @desc Whether your flight was domestic carriage (between States, or to, from or within a Territory) or international carriage that no Convention covers
      `the higher limit my ticket states` IS A NUMBER @desc The limit of liability your contract of carriage specifies (in dollars for a domestic flight, in SDRs otherwise); 0 if it states none
GIVETH A `Limit`
`The most the airline can be made to pay for my injury under the Regulations` MEANS
    CONSIDER `which carriage`
    WHEN `a domestic carrier, for carriage between States or to, from or within a Territory` THEN
        `a limit in Australian dollars` (`the s 31 limit from` `the s 31(1AA) amount` `the amount prescribed for s 31(1)` `the higher limit my ticket states`)
    WHEN `another Part IV carrier, for carriage between Australia and another country that no Convention covers` THEN
        `a limit in SDRs` (`the s 31 limit from` `the s 31(1B) number` `the SDRs prescribed for s 31(1A)` `the higher limit my ticket states`)

@export Under the Regulations in force, what is the most the airline can be made to pay for my baggage on a Part IV flight (s 31(2)-(4); regs 7(1), (2))?
GIVEN `the claim includes checked baggage` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim is, or includes, registered (checked) baggage
      `the higher limit my ticket states` IS A NUMBER @desc The baggage limit your contract of carriage specifies, in dollars; 0 if none
GIVETH A `Limit`
`The most the airline can be made to pay for my baggage under the Regulations` MEANS
    IF `the claim includes checked baggage`
    THEN `a limit in Australian dollars` (`the s 31 limit from` `the s 31(2A) amount` `the amount prescribed for s 31(2)` `the higher limit my ticket states`)
    ELSE `a limit in Australian dollars` (`the s 31 limit from` `the s 31(4) amount` `the amount prescribed for s 31(3)` `the higher limit my ticket states`)

§§ `ss 9C, 11A, 21A, 31, 40 -- has a regulation raised or displaced any limit`

DECLARE `Do the Regulations change my limit` IS ONE OF
    `no: the Regulations prescribe figures equal to the Act's for s 31 ($925,000, 480,000 SDRs, $3,000, $300), provide no indexation, and make no ticket or baggage check provisions under s 40 that could displace a limit`
    `no: the Regulations prescribe no number of SDRs for an Australian international carrier (ss 9C(1), 11A(1)(b), 21A(1)(b)), so its limit stays at the Convention's or the Act's figure`
    `the Regulations do not touch the 1999 Montreal Convention's own limits; an Article 24 revision, which is not deposited, may have raised them`

DECLARE `My flight's regime` IS ONE OF
    `Part IV of the Act`
    `the 1999 Montreal Convention, with an Australian international carrier`
    `the 1999 Montreal Convention, with another carrier`
    `a Warsaw Convention, with an Australian international carrier`

@export Do the Civil Aviation (Carriers' Liability) Regulations 2019 raise or displace the limit on my claim against the airline?
GIVEN `the regime` IS A `My flight's regime` @desc Which law governs your flight (see the question "Which law governs my flight") and whether the airline is an Australian international carrier
GIVETH A `Do the Regulations change my limit`
`Do the Regulations change my limit` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the regime`
    WHEN `Part IV of the Act` THEN `no: the Regulations prescribe figures equal to the Act's for s 31 ($925,000, 480,000 SDRs, $3,000, $300), provide no indexation, and make no ticket or baggage check provisions under s 40 that could displace a limit`
    WHEN `the 1999 Montreal Convention, with an Australian international carrier` THEN `no: the Regulations prescribe no number of SDRs for an Australian international carrier (ss 9C(1), 11A(1)(b), 21A(1)(b)), so its limit stays at the Convention's or the Act's figure`
    WHEN `a Warsaw Convention, with an Australian international carrier` THEN `no: the Regulations prescribe no number of SDRs for an Australian international carrier (ss 9C(1), 11A(1)(b), 21A(1)(b)), so its limit stays at the Convention's or the Act's figure`
    WHEN `the 1999 Montreal Convention, with another carrier` THEN `the Regulations do not touch the 1999 Montreal Convention's own limits; an Article 24 revision, which is not deposited, may have raised them`
